Since \(U_{\rm el}\) depends on \(\psi_{\rm eff}^{2}\), the sign of \(\psi_{\rm eff}\) does not appear explicitly in the energy; the term remains **positive** (repulsive) for any \(\theta\).  The magnitude of the repulsion is

\[
U_{\rm el}\propto\psi_{\rm eff}^{2}
=\psi_{0}^{2}\,(1-2\theta)^{2}
=\psi_{0}^{2}\,(2\theta-1)^{2},
\]

so that

* at the isoelectric point \(\theta_{\rm IEP}=0.5\) the effective potential vanishes \((\psi_{\rm eff}=0)\) and the electrostatic repulsion disappears;
* for \(\theta>0.5\) the particles become **overcharged** (\(\psi_{\rm eff}<0\)), but because the energy depends on the square of the potential the repulsion re‑appears with a strength that grows as \(|\psi_{\rm eff}|\).

Thus, the sign reversal of \(\psi_{\rm eff}\) at \(\theta>0.5\) does not change the functional form of the DLVO electrostatic term—it remains repulsive—but it produces a “re‑entrant’’ behavior: repulsion vanishes at the IEP and is restored (and even strengthened) when the particles are overcharged.

