\begin{minipage}{\linewidth}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Assumption of unchanged component spectra.} 
The Kerkhof–Moulijn model treats the composite spectrum as a simple weighted sum of the pure Pd 4d and Cu 2p spectra, implicitly assuming that the binding energies, peak shapes and intensities of each component remain the same after mixing. In reality, charge transfer and chemical bonding at the Pd/MOF interface cause shifts, broadening, and the appearance of new interfacial features that cannot be captured by a linear superposition.

\item \textbf{Use of bulk volume fractions for a surface‑sensitive probe.} 
X‑ray photoelectron spectroscopy probes only the top few nanometres of the material. The measured intensity is governed by the surface atomic (or area) fraction of each phase, the photo‑ionisation cross‑section, and attenuation of the emitted electrons, not by the bulk volume fractions ($f_{\rm Pd}=0.60$, $f_{\rm MOF}=0.40$) obtained from microscopy. Consequently the weighting in the linear‑combination model is inaccurate, leading to systematic errors in the extracted interfacial contribution.

\item \textbf{Neglect of attenuation and differing inelastic mean free paths.} 
Pd‑derived and Cu‑derived photoelectrons have different kinetic energies and thus different escape depths. The simple linear combination ignores depth‑dependent attenuation, which can preferentially suppress one component relative to the other and further distort the predicted composite spectrum.
\end{enumerate}
\end{minipage}
}
\]

