The rate \(r_{\,\text{C}_4\text{H}_8}\) is the formation rate of 1‑butene in \(\text{mol g}^{-1}\text{s}^{-1}\).

The surface‐bound ethylene coverage is given by the Langmuir isotherm  

\[
\theta_{C_2H_4}= \frac{K_{\rm ads}\,P_{C_2H_4}}{1+K_{\rm ads}P_{C_2H_4}}\;\approx\;K_{\rm ads}P_{C_2H_4}
\qquad (K_{\rm ads}=5\times10^{-3}\;{\rm atm}^{-1}) .
\]

---

### 1.  Rates of the elementary steps  

Insertion (first C–C bond formation)  

\[
k_{\rm ins}=A_{\rm ins}\,e^{-E_{a,{\rm ins}}/RT}\qquad\bigl[{\rm M^{-1}s^{-1}}\bigr]
\]

\[
r_{1}=k_{\rm ins}[C_2H_4]\;\theta_{C_2H_4}
\tag{1}
\]

β‑Hydride elimination from the C\(_4\) intermediate  

\[
k_{\rm elim}=A_{\rm elim}\,e^{-E_{a,{\rm elim}}/RT}\qquad\bigl[{\rm s^{-1}}\bigr]
\]

\[
r_{\rm elim}=k_{\rm elim}\,\theta_{C_4H_8}
\tag{2}
\]

Second insertion (the competing step that leads to the C\(_6\) product)  

\[
r_{2}=k_{\rm ins}[C_2H_4]\,\theta_{C_4H_8}
\tag{3}
\]

---

### 2.  Steady‑state for the C\(_4\) intermediate  

\[
\frac{d\theta_{C_4H_8}}{dt}=r_{1}-\bigl(k_{\rm elim}+k_{\rm ins}[C_2H_4]\bigr)\theta_{C_4H_8}=0
\]

\[
\Rightarrow\qquad 
\theta_{C_4H_8}= \frac{r_{1}}{k_{\rm elim}+k_{\rm ins}[C_2H_4]}
\tag{4}
\]

---

### 3.  Rates of product formation  

\[
\begin{aligned}
r_{\rm 1\!-\!butene}&=r_{\rm elim}=k_{\rm elim}\,\theta_{C_4H_8}
                 =\frac{k_{\rm elim}\,r_{1}}{k_{\rm elim}+k_{\rm ins}[C_2H_4]},\\[4pt]
r_{\rm C_6}&=r_{2}=k_{\rm ins}[C_2H_4]\,\theta_{C_4H_8}
            =\frac{k_{\rm ins}[C_2H_4]\,r_{1}}{k_{\rm elim}+k_{\rm ins}[C_2H_4]} .
\end{aligned}
\tag{5}
\]

---

### 4.  Ratio of the two product rates  

Dividing the two expressions in (5) gives a simple result in which the
coverage term \(r_{1}\) cancels:

\[
\boxed{\frac{r_{\rm 1\!-\!butene}}{r_{\rm C_6}}
      =\frac{k_{\rm elim}}{k_{\rm ins}[C_2H_4]}}
\tag{6}
\]

Using the Arrhenius forms for the rate constants and the ideal‑gas
relation \([C_2H_4]=P_{C_2H_4}/(RT)\),

\[
\boxed{
\frac{r_{\rm 1\!-\!butene}}{r_{\rm C_6}}
= \frac{A_{\rm elim}}{A_{\rm ins}}\;
   \frac{RT}{P_{C_2H_4}}\;
   \exp\!\Big[-\frac{E_{a,{\rm elim}}-E_{a,{\rm ins}}}{RT}\Big]
}
\tag{7}
\]

Thus the selectivity toward 1‑butene versus the C\(_6\) α‑olefin is
controlled solely by the competition between β‑hydride elimination
and a second ethylene insertion, and depends on temperature,
ethylene partial pressure, and the kinetic parameters \(A\) and
\(E_a\) of the insertion and elimination steps.

