*Explanation.*  
The objective maximises the expected net‑present‑value (NPV) of the plant over the 20‑year horizon using the means of the log‑normal price distributions.  
Binary variables \(x_{i,t}\) indicate whether retrofit \(i\) is installed in year \(t\); each retrofit can be installed at most once.  
The HEN stages must be installed sequentially, enforced by the ordering constraints.  
If the CO₂‑capture retrofit is installed, a recycle fraction \(f_{CO2}\) (continuous, ≤ 1) is chosen; otherwise \(f_{CO2}=0\).  
Methanol production \(Q_{MeOH,t}\) follows the given quadratic yield‑increase function once the CC retrofit is in place; raw‑material consumption scales with production (1.2 t RM per t MeOH).  
CO₂‑avoidance credit \(R_{CO2,t}\) is proportional to the amount of CO₂ recycled.  
Electricity exported to the grid consists of the 5 MW cogeneration unit (if installed) plus electricity generated from recovered H₂ via the PSA‑fuel‑cell block (50 % conversion efficiency).  
Utility cost is reduced by each HEN stage by the amount \(\Delta U_k\).  
Capital expenditures are deducted in the year of installation.  

This mixed‑integer (non‑linear) programme provides the optimal retrofit schedule and the optimal CO₂‑recycle fraction that maximise the plant’s expected NPV.

