\boxed{
\text{In the d‑band model the strength of chemisorption correlates with the position of the metal d‑band centre } \varepsilon_d \text{ relative to the Fermi level }E_F\text{: a higher (closer to }E_F\text{) d‑band centre yields stronger metal–adsorbate bonding.}\\
\text{Sn is a more electropositive element than the transition metal M. When Sn atoms are introduced into the cluster they donate electron density to the neighbouring M atoms (ligand effect). This charge transfer increases the d‑band filling of M, which shifts the d‑band centre downwards (to more negative energies) relative to }E_F.\\
\text{A down‑shift of }\varepsilon_d\text{ reduces the overlap between the metal d‑states and the frontier orbitals of the adsorbates (C}_{2}\text{H}_{4},\ H,\ C_{2}\text{H}_{6}\text{), weakening the metal–adsorbate bond and making the adsorption energies less exothermic.}\\
\text{In addition, alloying with the larger Sn atoms expands the lattice (strain effect) and narrows the d‑band, which also lowers }\varepsilon_d\text{. The observed change from high‑spin (large exchange splitting) in the monometallic clusters to low‑spin (S=0) in the bimetallic clusters further reduces exchange splitting, contributing to a lower d‑band centre.}\\
\text{Consequently, the (MSn)}_{N}\text{ clusters exhibit weaker adsorption of C}_{2}\text{H}_{4}, H, and C}_{2}\text{H}_{6}\text{ than the pure M}_{N}\text{ clusters, consistent with the down‑shifted d‑band centre caused by Sn incorporation.}
}
