For the very early stage the concentration disturbance in the solid is confined to a diffusion layer of thickness  

\[
\delta(t)\sim \sqrt{D(pH_{s})\,t}\qquad (t\ll\tau ) .
\]

Hence the concentration profile can be approximated by the classic “error‑function’’ solution for a semi‑infinite medium with a (slowly) varying surface concentration.  To leading order the gradient at the surface is  

\[
\left.\frac{\partial C}{\partial x}\right|_{x=0}
\;\simeq\;
\frac{C_{s}(t)-C_{0}}{\delta(t)}
=
\frac{C_{s}(t)-C_{0}}{\sqrt{D(pH_{s})\,t}} .
\]

The diffusive flux out of the solid (positive into the solution) is therefore  

\[
J(0,t)=-D(pH_{s})\Bigl.\frac{\partial C}{\partial x}\Bigr|_{x=0}
\;\simeq\;
\frac{C_{0}-C_{s}(t)}{\sqrt{\pi\,D(pH_{s})\,t}} .
\tag{1}
\]

(The factor \(1/\sqrt{\pi}\) follows from the exact error‑function solution for a step change; it is retained because it gives the correct early‑time magnitude.)

The aqueous concentration obeys the mass balance  

\[
V\frac{dC_{a}}{dt}=A\,J(0,t)
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\frac{dC_{a}}{dt}
=\frac{A}{V}\,
\frac{C_{0}-C_{s}(t)}{\sqrt{\pi\,D(pH_{s})\,t}} .
\tag{2}
\]

At early times the aqueous concentration is still very small, so that  

\[
C_{s}(t)=K\,[C_{a}(t)]^{\,n}\ll C_{0},
\]

and we may replace \(C_{0}-C_{s}\) by \(C_{0}\) in (2).  Integration from 0 to \(t\) gives  

\[
C_{a}(t)\;\approx\;
\frac{A\,C_{0}}{V\sqrt{\pi\,D(pH_{s})}}
\int_{0}^{t}\!t^{-1/2}\,dt
=
\frac{2A\,C_{0}}{V\sqrt{\pi\,D(pH_{s})}}\;\sqrt{t}.
\tag{3}
\]

Finally the surface concentration follows from the Freundlich isotherm  

\[
C_{s}(t)=K\,[C_{a}(t)]^{\,n}
\;\approx\;
K\Biggl[\frac{2A\,C_{0}}{V\sqrt{\pi\,D(pH_{s})}}\Biggr]^{\!n}
\,t^{\,n/2}.
\tag{4}
\]

Using the pH‑dependent diffusion coefficient  

\[
D(pH_{s})=D_{0}\,\exp\!\bigl[\beta\,(pH_{s}-pH_{0})\bigr],
\]

the early‑time expressions become  

\[
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
C_{a}(t)&\;\approx\;
\frac{2A\,C_{0}}{V\sqrt{\pi\,D_{0}\,e^{\beta(pH_{s}-pH_{0})}}}\;
\sqrt{t},\\[4pt]
C_{s}(t)&\;\approx\;
K\Biggl[\frac{2A\,C_{0}}{V\sqrt{\pi\,D_{0}\,e^{\beta(pH_{s}-pH_{0})}}}\Biggr]^{\!n}
\,t^{\,n/2}.
\end{aligned}}
\]

These formulas are valid as long as \(t\ll\tau\), i.e. while the diffusion layer \(\delta(t)\) remains thin and \(C_{s}(t)\ll C_{0}\).

