The heat of immersion measured for the 300 K pretreatment is assumed to arise only from the formation of the strongly‑bound H₂O layer.  
For a mass of sample equal to 1 g the heat released is  

\[
Q_{1}\;(\mathrm{J\,g^{-1}})=\frac{\theta_{1}\,N_{\rm sites}\,\Delta H_{1}}{N_{\rm A}},
\]

where \(N_{\rm sites}=S=5.0\times10^{18}\) surface sites g⁻¹ and \(N_{\rm A}=6.022\times10^{23}\) mol⁻¹.  
Thus

\[
\theta_{1}= \frac{Q_{1}\,N_{\rm A}}{S\,\Delta H_{1}}
          =\frac{Q_{1}\;(6.022\times10^{23})}{(5.0\times10^{18})\,\Delta H_{1}}
          =\frac{Q_{1}\;(1.2044\times10^{5})}{\Delta H_{1}} .
\]

Substituting the measured heats of immersion gives the coverage fractions in terms of the (unknown but common) adsorption enthalpy \(\Delta H_{1}\):

\[
\begin{aligned}
\theta_{1,\;{\rm CaF_{2}}}&=\frac{120\;(1.2044\times10^{5})}{\Delta H_{1}}
                           =\frac{1.45\times10^{7}}{\Delta H_{1}},\\[4pt]
\theta_{1,\;{\rm SrF_{2}}}&=\frac{115\;(1.2044\times10^{5})}{\Delta H_{1}}
                           =\frac{1.39\times10^{7}}{\Delta H_{1}},\\[4pt]
\theta_{1,\;{\rm BaF_{2}}}&=\frac{110\;(1.2044\times10^{5})}{\Delta H_{1}}
                           =\frac{1.33\times10^{7}}{\Delta H_{1}} .
\end{aligned}
\]

Hence, for a given \(\Delta H_{1}\) the fractions of surface sites covered by the strongly adsorbed water layer are

\[
\boxed{\theta_{1,\mathrm{Ca}}=\dfrac{1.45\times10^{7}}{\Delta H_{1}},\;
       \theta_{1,\mathrm{Sr}}=\dfrac{1.39\times10^{7}}{\Delta H_{1}},\;
       \theta_{1,\mathrm{Ba}}=\dfrac{1.33\times10^{7}}{\Delta H_{1}} }.
\]

(If a typical strong‑water adsorption enthalpy of, e.g., \(\Delta H_{1}\approx1.5\times10^{5}\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}\) is assumed, the numerical values become \(\theta_{1}\approx96,\;92,\;88\), i.e. far larger than unity, indicating multilayer adsorption.)

