Under the stated approximations the total guest–water interaction energy is obtained by adding the Lennard‑Jones contribution from each of the \(N\) water sites.  
For a water molecule placed on the circum‑circle of radius \(d_{\text{ring}}/2\) and a guest at a distance \(r\) from the centre, the distance between the guest and the \(j^{\rm th}\) water oxygen is obtained from the law of cosines

\[
r_{j}(r)=\sqrt{\,r^{2}+\Bigl(\frac{d_{\text{ring}}}{2}\Bigr)^{2}
-\,r\,d_{\text{ring}}\cos\theta_{j}\,},\qquad 
\theta_{j}= \frac{2\pi j}{N}\;(j=1,\dots ,N).
\]

Using the Lorentz–Berthelot mixing rules,
\[
\sigma_{gw}= \frac{\sigma_{\text{guest}}+\sigma_{\text{water}}}{2},
\qquad
\varepsilon_{gw}= \sqrt{\varepsilon_{\text{guest}}\,
\varepsilon_{\text{water}}},
\]
which are the same for every site, the total interaction energy becomes  

\[
\boxed{
U(r)=\sum_{j=1}^{N}U_{ij}(r_{j})
=4\varepsilon_{gw}\sum_{j=1}^{N}
\Bigg[
\Bigg(\frac{\sigma_{gw}}{r_{j}(r)}\Bigg)^{12}
-\Bigg(\frac{\sigma_{gw}}{r_{j}(r)}\Bigg)^{6}
\Bigg]
}
\]

or, explicitly in terms of \(r\) and \(d_{\text{ring}}\),

\[
\boxed{
U(r)=4\varepsilon_{gw}\sum_{j=1}^{N}
\left[
