\frac{\partial\theta_i}{\partial K_{a,i}} >0 .
\]

A higher \(K_{a,i}\) raises the numerator for species *i* and also enlarges the denominator \(D=1+K_{a,\mathrm{CO_2}}P_{\mathrm{CO_2}}+K_{a,\mathrm{CO}}P_{\mathrm{CO}}+\sqrt{K_{a,\mathrm{H_2}}P_{\mathrm{H_2}}}\).  The net result is a larger fraction of surface sites occupied by the promoted species (CO₂*, CO*, H*) and a concomitant reduction of vacant sites \(\theta_{*}\) and of the other adsorbates.  

For the given feed ( \(P_{\rm tot}=20\) bar, \(P_{\rm CO_2}=6\) bar, \(P_{\rm CO}=2\) bar, \(P_{\rm H_2}=12\) bar) the numerical coverages increase systematically from the 0 %‑K case (\(K_{a,\rm CO_2}=0.05\), \(K_{a,\rm CO}=0.30\), \(K_{a,\rm H_2}=0.10\)) to the 2 %‑K case (\(K_{a,\rm CO_2}=0.20\), \(K_{a,\rm CO}=0.60\), \(K_{a,\rm H_2}=0.20\)), illustrating how potassium promotion shifts the surface composition toward stronger CO₂, CO and H adsorption, which in turn influences the rates of RWGS, FTS and methanation.

