\[
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\mu_i^{\rm ex}(H,R)&=\mu_i^{\rm ex,b}+k_{\!B}T\ln\!\frac{y_i\rho_{b}}{\displaystyle\frac{\langle N_i\rangle}{V_{p}}}   \\
&=\underbrace{\sum_{j=1}^{2}\rho_j\int_{V_{p}}\!\big[g_{ij}({\bf r})-1\big]\;u_{ij}(r)\;d{\bf r}}_{\displaystyle\text{fluid–fluid (pair) contribution}}
\;+\;
\underbrace{\int_{V_{p}}\!\rho_i({\bf r})\,U_{iw}({\bf r})\;d{\bf r}}_{\displaystyle\text{fluid–wall contribution}} .
\end{aligned}
}
\]

Here  

* \(V_{p}\) is the accessible pore volume (slit: \(V_{p}=A\,H\); cylinder: \(V_{p}=\pi R^{2}L\); sphere: \(V_{p}=4\pi R^{3}/3\)).  
* \(\rho_j\) is the average number density of species \(j\) in the pore, \(\rho_j=\langle N_j\rangle/V_{p}\).  
* \(u_{ij}(r)\) is the Lennard‑Jones pair potential between species \(i\) and \(j\).  
* \(g_{ij}({\bf r})\) is the pair‑distribution function in the confined fluid.  
* \(U_{iw}({\bf r})\) is the external (particle‑wall) potential.  For the three idealised geometries one may use the integrated 9‑3 LJ wall potential  

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{slit:}\qquad 
U_{iw}(z)&=2\pi\rho_w\epsilon_{iw}\sigma_{iw}^{3}
\Big[\frac{2}{15}\Big(\frac{\sigma_{iw}}{z}\Big)^{9}
-\Big(\frac{\sigma_{iw}}{z}\Big)^{3}\Big]
+2\pi\rho_w\epsilon_{iw}\sigma_{iw}^{3}
\Big[\frac{2}{15}\Big(\frac{\sigma_{iw}}{H-z}\Big)^{9}
-\Big(\frac{\sigma_{iw}}{H-z}\Big)^{3}\Big],
\\[4pt]
\text{cylinder:}\qquad 
U_{iw}(r)&=2\pi\rho_w\epsilon_{iw}\sigma_{iw}^{3}
\Big[\frac{2}{15}\Big(\frac{\sigma_{iw}}{R-r}\Big)^{9}
-\Big(\frac{\sigma_{iw}}{R-r}\Big)^{3}\Big],
\\[4pt]
\text{sphere:}\qquad 
U_{iw}(r)&=2\pi\rho_w\epsilon_{iw}\sigma_{iw}^{3}
\Big[\frac{2}{15}\Big(\frac{\sigma_{iw}}{R-r}\Big)^{9}
-\Big(\frac{\sigma_{iw}}{R-r}\Big)^{3}\Big],
\end{aligned}
\]

with \(\rho_w\) the wall atom density, \(\epsilon_{iw}\) and \(\sigma_{iw}\) the LJ wall parameters for species \(i\).  

The first term in the boxed expression accounts for the excess chemical potential arising from fluid‑fluid (pair) interactions, while the second term captures the contribution of the confining wall through the external potential \(U_{iw}\).  Equality of the bulk and pore grand‑canonical chemical potentials \(\mu_i^{\rm b}=\mu_i^{\rm p}\) leads to the logarithmic ideal‑gas correction shown in the first line.  This is the desired expression for the excess chemical potential \(\mu_i^{\rm ex}(H,R)\) of each species in a slit, cylindrical or spherical nanopore.

