The two elementary Langmuir–Hinshelwood pathways are  

\[
\begin{aligned}
r_{O_2}&=k_{O_2}\;S_{\rm eff}\;
          \frac{K_{O_2}P_{O_2}}{1+K_{O_2}P_{O_2}+K_{NO_2}P_{NO_2}},\\[4pt]
r_{NO_2}&=k_{NO_2}\;S_{BET}\;
          \frac{K_{NO_2}P_{NO_2}}{1+K_{O_2}P_{O_2}+K_{NO_2}P_{NO_2}},
\end{aligned}
\]

with  

\[
S_{\rm eff}=S_{BET}\,(1-\theta_O),\qquad 
k_{O_2}=A_{O_2}\exp\!\Big(-\frac{E_{a,O_2}}{RT}\Big),\qquad
k_{NO_2}=A_{NO_2}\exp\!\Big(-\frac{E_{a,NO_2}}{RT}\Big).
\]

Adding the two contributions gives the total mass‑loss rate

\[
r_{\rm tot}=r_{O_2}+r_{NO_2}
           =\frac{k_{O_2}\,S_{BET}(1-\theta_O)K_{O_2}P_{O_2}
                 +k_{NO_2}\,S_{BET}K_{NO_2}P_{NO_2}}
                {1+K_{O_2}P_{O_2}+K_{NO_2}P_{NO_2}} .
\]

Substituting the Arrhenius forms for the kinetic constants yields an explicit
function of temperature \(T\) and the micro‑structural parameters of a sample:

\[
\boxed{
r_{\rm tot}(T;d_p,S_{BET},\theta_O,N)=
\frac{
A_{O_2}\,e^{-E_{a,O_2}/(RT)}\,S_{BET}\,(1-\theta_O)\,K_{O_2}P_{O_2}
\;+\;
A_{NO_2}\,e^{-E_{a,NO_2}/(RT)}\,S_{BET}\,K_{NO_2}P_{NO_2}
}{
1+K_{O_2}P_{O_2}+K_{NO_2}P_{NO_2}
}}
\]

The only sample‑specific quantities that appear are the specific surface
area \(S_{BET}\) and the fraction of oxidised surface sites \(\theta_O\).
The primary‑particle diameter \(d_p\) and the average stack height \(N\) 
affect the rate indirectly through their influence on the measured
\(S_{BET}\); for a simple geometric model one may write  

\[
S_{BET}\;\approx\;\frac{6}{\rho\,d_p\,N},
\]

with \(\rho\) the soot bulk density (≈ 2 g cm\(^{-3}\)).  Inserting this
relation into the boxed expression makes the dependence on \(d_p\) and
\(N\) explicit.  The resulting formula can be used for any of the three
samples (A, B, C) by inserting the appropriate values of
\(d_p,\;S_{BET},\;\theta_O\) and \(N\).

