**Derivation**

The net amount of carbon (in g C) that can be sequestered by a particle of radius \(r\) is  

\[
C_{\text{net}}(r)=0.28\,\frac{M_{\text{tot}}}{\rho_{p}}\;f_{\text{diss}}(r)-\frac{E_{\text{grind}}(r)}{\eta}.
\tag{1}
\]

---

### 1.  Dissolution fraction \(f_{\text{diss}}(r)\)

The residence time of a particle while it is inside the mixed layer is  

\[
t_{\text{res}}=\frac{H}{v_{s}(r)} .
\]

The dissolution flux per unit area is assumed constant:

\[
R_{\text{diss}}(r)=k\;(1-\Omega)\simeq k .
\]

Hence the fraction of the particle mass that dissolves while it traverses the mixed layer is  

\[
\begin{aligned}
f_{\text{diss}}(r)
&=\frac{4\pi r^{2}R_{\text{diss}}(r)\,t_{\text{res}}}
       {\tfrac{4}{3}\pi r^{3}\rho_{p}}               \\
&=\frac{3\,R_{\text{diss}}(r)\,H}{r\,\rho_{p}\,v_{s}(r)} .
\tag{2}
\end{aligned}
\]

The Stokes terminal velocity for a sphere of radius \(r\) is  

\[
v_{s}(r)=\frac{2\,g\,(\rho_{p}-\rho_{w})\,r^{2}}{9\,\mu}
        \equiv A\,r^{2},
\qquad 
A=\frac{2g(\rho_{p}-\rho_{w})}{9\mu}.
\tag{3}
\]

Insert (3) into (2) and use \(R_{\text{diss}}(r)=k\):

\[
\begin{aligned}
f_{\text{diss}}(r)
&=\frac{3kH}{r\rho_{p}\,A\,r^{2}}
 =\frac{3kH}{A\,\rho_{p}\,r^{3}} .
\end{aligned}
\tag{4}
\]

Writing the constant \(A\) explicitly,

\[
f_{\text{diss}}(r)=\frac{3kH\,9\mu}
                       {2g(\rho_{p}-\rho_{w})\,\rho_{p}\,r^{3}}
               =\frac{27\mu k H}
                       {2g(\rho_{p}-\rho_{w})\,\rho_{p}\,r^{3}} .
\tag{5}
\]

---

### 2.  Grinding energy

The grinding energy required for a particle of radius \(r\) is  

\[
E_{\text{grind}}(r)=\frac{\alpha}{r}.
\tag{6}
\]

---

### 3.  Net carbon sequestration

Substituting (5) and (6) into (1) gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
C_{\text{net}}(r)
&=0.28\,\frac{M_{\text{tot}}}{\rho_{p}}\;
   \frac{27\mu k H}
        {2g(\rho_{p}-\rho_{w})\,\rho_{p}\,r^{3}}
   -\frac{\alpha}{\eta\,r}  \\
&=\frac{0.28\;27\;\mu\;k\;H\;M_{\text{tot}}}
        {2\,g\,(\rho_{p}-\rho_{w})\,\rho_{p}^{2}\;r^{3}}
   -\frac{\alpha}{\eta\,r}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{7}
\]

All symbols now have numerical values:

\[
\begin{aligned}
&k = 1.0\times10^{-9}\;{\rm m\,s^{-1}}, &
\mu = 1.0\times10^{-3}\;{\rm Pa\,s}, &
H = 50\;{\rm m},\\[2pt]
&M_{\text{tot}} = 1.0\times10^{12}\;{\rm kg}, &
\rho_{p}=3300\;{\rm kg\,m^{-3}}, &
\rho_{w}=1025\;{\rm kg\,m^{-3}},\\[2pt]
&g = 9.81\;{\rm m\,s^{-2}}, &
\alpha = 5.0\times10^{5}\;{\rm J\,m}, &
\eta = 3.6\times10^{8}\;{\rm J\,g^{-1}} .
\end{aligned}
\]

First compute the constant that multiplies \(r^{-3}\):

\[
\begin{aligned}
C_{1}
&=\frac{0.28\;27\;\mu\;k\;H\;M_{\text{tot}}}
        {2\,g\,(\rho_{p}-\rho_{w})\,\rho_{p}^{2}}\\[2pt]
&=\frac{0.28\times27\times(1.0\times10^{-3})\times(1.0\times10^{-9})
        \times50\times(1.0\times10^{12})}
        {2\times9.81\times(3300-1025)\times(3300)^{2}}\\[2pt]
&=\frac{3.78\times10^{2}}{4.8608\times10^{11}}
 \;\approx\;7.78\times10^{-10}\;.
\end{aligned}
\]

Next the constant that multiplies \(r^{-1}\):

\[
C_{2}= \frac{\alpha}{\eta}
      =\frac{5.0\times10^{5}}{3.6\times10^{8}}
      =1.389\times10^{-3}\;.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{%
C_{\text{net}}(r)=\frac{7.78\times10^{-10}}{r^{3}}
                 -\frac{1.389\times10^{-3}}{r}
                 \;\;\text{[g C]},\qquad r\ \text{in metres}.
}
\tag{8}
\]

Equation (8) is the explicit net‑carbon‑sequestration expression as a function of the particle radius \(r\) only, with all given constants substituted.

