**Explanation**

* The three resistances are in series, so the total resistance per unit interfacial area is  

\[
R_{\rm tot}= \frac{\delta}{D_{g}}+\frac{1}{k_{i}}+\frac{R}{D_{b}} .
\]

* Converting the pressure driving force to a concentration difference
\(c_{\rm eq}-c_{\infty}=(p_{\rm eq}-p_{\infty})/(R_{g}T)\) gives the
area‑averaged molar flux  

\[
J_{A}= \frac{p_{\rm eq}-p_{\infty}}{R_{g}T\,R_{\rm tot}} .
\]

* Multiplying by the droplet surface area \(4\pi R^{2}\) yields the total
molar flux \(J\) shown above.

* The loss of solute mass per unit time is \(-J\,M_{s}\) ( \(M_{s}\) =
molar mass of the volatile solute).  Using the volume–radius relation
\(V=\tfrac{4}{3}\pi R^{3}\) and \(\rho_{\ell}=m/V\) gives  

\[
4\pi R^{2}\,\frac{{\rm d}R}{{\rm d}t}= -\frac{J\,M_{s}}{\rho_{\ell}} ,
\]

which leads directly to the differential equation for \(R(t)\) written
above.  The equilibrium vapor pressure at the surface follows the
Raoult‑Kelvin expression  

\[
p_{\rm eq}=x\,\gamma_{s}\,p^{*}_{s}
           \exp\!\Big(\frac{2\sigma M}{\rho_{\ell}R_{g}T\,R}\Big),
\]

so the driving force \((p_{\rm eq}-p_{\infty})\) contains the
composition‑dependent term and the curvature (Kelvin) correction.  

