*Explanation:*  
Using the Anderson–Schulz–Flory law, the weight fraction of C₅⁺ hydrocarbons is  
\(\displaystyle S_{C_{5+}}=\sum_{n=5}^{\infty}W_n
   =(1-\alpha)\alpha^{4}\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\alpha^{k}
   =\alpha^{4}\).  

The methane selectivity follows directly from the ASF distribution as \(W_1=1-\alpha\).  
The CO₂ selectivity is the rate of the water‑gas‑shift step relative to the total rate and is written in terms of the kinetic parameters of step 7.  

Thus the optimization problem is to choose the operating conditions (through their effect on the kinetic parameters) that maximize \(\alpha^{4}\) while satisfying the methane (≤ 2 %) and CO₂ (≤ 12 %) constraints.  The optimal solution occurs at the largest feasible α; the methane constraint gives \(\alpha\ge0.98\).  If the CO₂ constraint is less restrictive, the optimum is \(\alpha^{*}=0.98\) giving a liquid‑hydrocarbon selectivity of \((0.98)^{4}\approx0.922\) (≈ 92 %).

