**Comment:**  
An \(n\) value of ≈ 3.3 indicates that the ORR proceeds mainly via the four‑electron pathway but with a substantial two‑electron (peroxide) side reaction (≈ 33 % HO₂⁻ formation).  

**Effect of pyridinic N:**  
Pyridinic N sites have a lower free‑energy barrier (ΔG_RDS = 0.35 eV) and thus a much larger rate constant (≈ 10 s⁻¹) than graphitic N (≈ 0.2 s⁻¹) according to the BEP relationship. Raising the fraction of pyridinic N (f_pyr) increases the proportion of the more active sites, raising the overall kinetic rate constant and favouring the direct four‑electron reduction of O₂. Consequently, an increase in f_pyr would be expected to raise \(n\) toward the ideal value of 4, reducing peroxide production.

